  "subtitle": "An optical bridge between real brushes and digital pixels — paint directly into a computer with your hands",
  "why_scout_cares": "In 1985, Richard Greene built a transparent glass prism that let artists paint with real brushes, fingers, or any light-colored object directly into a computer's frame buffer. A video camera underneath the prism saw only the points of optical contact — via frustrated total internal reflection — and digitized them in real time. You could use a Japanese sumi-e brush, your thumb, or a palette knife, and the computer would render every stroke. This was multi-touch drawing with pressure sensitivity, using real physical tools, 20 years before FTIR multi-touch screens appeared in consumer products. The ACM SIGGRAPH paper is rich with technical detail, artistic philosophy, and wonder at what a computer could become when you removed the keyboard and mouse and let people work with their hands on a glowing surface.",
